        "id": "ch9_10-1",
        "text": "The last reboot command\nThe last reboot command in Linux is a simple yet crucial utility that provides information about\nthe system\u2019s reboot history. It displays the timestamps of previous system reboots, along with the time\nsince the last reboot. This information is helpful for system administrators to track system availability\nand uptime. By regularly checking the last reboot timestamps, administrators can quickly identify\nif the system experienced any unexpected shutdowns or reboots. This information can be vital for\ndiagnosing potential hardware or software issues that may be causing system instability.\nThe primary use of the last reboot command is for historical analysis and tracking system\navailability. System administrators can utilize this information to calculate the system\u2019s mean time\nbetween failures ( MTBF ) and mean time to repair ( MTTR ), which are essential metrics for evaluating\nsystem reliability.\nTo display the system\u2019s reboot history, we use the following command:\nFigure 9.19 \u2013 Using last reboot to display the system\u2019s reboot history",
